A||||26||cardealer||V8 swap this weekend ! (how long do you think it will take?)||||1||
Z||000000||cardealer||03-23-2001||10:46 PM||||finally going to do the swap this weekend, i think i finally got everything i need. if not i have 5 auto parts stores all within a mile of my house. i already have the fuel pump changed in the tank (thanks archie for the advice)and a 5 core raditor with electric fans and the auto trans lines rerouted (no, the 5 core was not a direct fit). after market gauges mounted and everything unbolted and ready to drop except the cradle bolts. wiring harness undone and pushed through the firewall. basicly everything ready to drop the old v6 out. any bets on how long it will take to the point i can drive it?<P><BR>if the is anyone with vast knowledge on v8 swaps and is going near their computer this weekend please send me your email so if i encounter any problems i can seek some support ! (tonypaul@fastestcar.com)<BR>thanks<P>i will post pics on monday of what i got accomplished over the weekend.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||03-23-2001||11:29 PM||||I have heard it said that to calculate the amount of time required for a project, first multiply your "estimate" by two, then change to the next highest unit of measure. <BR>In other words, a "two hour" job will take four weeks, etc.<BR>Just kidding. Hope that's not the case. Best of luck!<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||12
Z||000002||KRMFiero||03-24-2001||07:38 AM||||Well it took me about 1 day to get my Duke out and off the Tranny (myself) i spend about an hour cleaning the cradle/tranny, well i'm not much farther then that, what engine are you putting in? bubbajoexx says for the Caddy swap its about 10 hours<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||03-24-2001||08:12 AM||||Ill bet you wont be driving it, finished anyway, this weekend. Ill almost guarantee something wont fit rite, or youll need some weird part that NOBODY has. Itll take most of the weekend just to figure out an exhaust system. Ask Lisa how many things go rite when you plan em. Anyway I wish you luck and it will be a fun car when it is done. I spent 1 1/2 days trying to find the right map sensor for mine. Even all the dealers had the wrong part number.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tina||03-24-2001||08:26 AM||||Well it all depends  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Got air tools at your house, a partsrunner,<BR>and lots of expierience?<BR>I would say however long you thought it would take you. Double that time.<P>Lots of luck,<BR>Tina<P>BTW You never mentioned what kind of V-8<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||03-24-2001||07:43 PM||||Unless you've done this before and have a prfessionally equipped shop with lots of able helpers, it's going to be a least two or three weekends before it's in running condition and then a couple more weeks sorting out all the bugs. At least that's how my projects go.||||reg||1
Z||000006||cardealer||03-24-2001||11:18 PM||||i cant belive all that i got done today ! new engine is in and a lot of the little things are hooked up again. it took about 6 hours take out the 2.8 and bolt up the 350 and run remote oil lines, starter, attach carb, another 4 hours on wiring harness, shifter cable, fuel lines, ect, and figuring out how i am going to do some of the things like alt pulley, electric water pump,ect. since i pretty much made my own kit most of this has been 1/2 calculations and 1/2 guessing, but everything fit perfect "so-far". i hope i get as much done tomorrow as i did today, i am figuring i have two more full days worth of work before i can take down for the exhaust. this has been much easier than i expected but... its not over yet !<P>more sunday night maybe some pics !<P>chevy corvette 350- bored 30,vette heads ported & polished, forged pistons, comp 270 cam, edelbrock rpm intake, edelbrock 750 carb, ect.... ||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||03-24-2001||11:24 PM||||Excellent! You must be living right or just plain lucky. That engine will be awesome, but I don't understand why you're using a 750 carb. Too big, you might have a low end bog unless you're an excellent carb tuner. Those 750's are for big blocks not 350 small blocks.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/batkid3.gif">    <B>BATBOY</B><BR>1985 SE auto with 350 Chevy V8 (Batmobile),<BR>suspension mods, lowered, GA brakes, battery<BR>relocated up front, stereo mods, etc...||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric||03-24-2001||11:26 PM||||Wow, I'm impressed.  I guess my excuse is I never have all the parts lined up before I begin something like this.<P>Isn't that a lot of carb?  Must be one good breathing small block.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Taijiguy||03-25-2001||08:25 PM||||Actually, Holley recommends the 750 for "built" small blocks. Just read that today as a matter of fact. A 350 motor with a redline of 8000 rpm with a 90% volumetric efficiency would use about 720 cfm. A 750 would be ideal it seems  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> (Of course, all that is subject to many variables, but it does fit a "typical" scenario)||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||03-25-2001||09:58 PM||||A "built" 350 that can turn 8 grand is a full bore racing engine that will have roller cam, true roller rockers and all those toys. Plus, these engines never run under 3 grand rpm. Look at Cardealer's engine specs: Comp Cams 270 and Edelbrock RPM dual plane... max rpm of 6 grand and if you want it to "live long and prosper" better make that shift redline 5 grand. Not quite the same. From personal experience, a 600 to 650 cfm is all need if you want to retain excellent low to mid range torque and throttle response. I'm speaking from experience here, but if you don't believe me, read John Lingenfelter's book on modifying Chevy small blocks... funny, he says the same thing.||||reg||1
Z||000011||cardealer||03-26-2001||12:05 AM||||another day... not as good as yesterday, the wife wanted me to go to church this morning (and i thought i could use all the help i could get) so i didnt get started untill after 1:00pm started on attaching the struts and brakes first side went great. the second side a differant story... as i was trying to get strut in and the cv joint fell out of the side of the tranny. the little bearings along with a glob of grease fell to the ground, wasted 2 hours going to 4 differant part stores trying to find just the cv joint but no luck. i will buy the whole axel tomorrow for $55.00. if it havnt been for the late start today and the wasted time searching for parts i would be very close to being done. still got a lot done today,i should be able to finsh everything next weekend.<P>as far as the carb setup, this is the same carb size as i ran on my v8 s10 blazer with the same engine setup minus hedders. i was very happy with it, but if turns out to be to much i will take it back and get a 600.<P>i loaded a few photos from this weekend, check them out...<BR>ive done some things differant than most swaps ive seen :<BR>mounted electric waterpump in the trunk and plumbed to the front of the engine.<BR>i am moving the battery into the trunk area.<BR>a few other things but i dont have pics yet, gotta wait till next weekend!||||reg||10
